# Bit representation
All data in computer programs is internally stored as bits.
Bit representation of a number is either signed or unsigned.
First bit in a signed representation is the sign of the number and remaining `n-1` bits contain the
magnitude of the number.
Opposite number of a number is calculated by two's complement - inverting all the bits and then 
increasing by one.
Unsigned representation can only be used to represent positive numbers.
If the number is a larger than the uppoer bound of the bit representation, the number will overflow 
and wrap around.

# Bit operations
and(&), or(|), xor(^), not(~), bit shift(<<, >>)

`__builtin_clz(x)`: number of zeroes at the beginning of the number

`__builtin_ctz(x)`: number of zeroes at the end of the number

`__builtin_popcount(x)`: the number of ones in the number

`__builtin_parity(x)`: parity(even or odd) of the number of ones

# Representing sets

x-bit `int` number can represent any subset of the set $${0, 1,\ldots, x}$$

## Iterating through subsets

Iterate through all subsets with exactly `k` elements of set $${0, 1, \ldots, n-1}$$
```cpp
for (int b = 0; b < (1<<n); b++) {
  // process subset b
}
```

Iterate through the subsets of a set `x`
```cpp
int b = 0;
do {
  // process subset b
} while (b=(b-x)&x);
```

# Technique
Sometimes it is possible to convert problem over permutations to problem over subsets.
